course: adult-life
lesson: 46
title: Tabloids, «sources close to» and the photo that tells a story
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Grown-ups love a bit of celebrity gossip: the magazine by the shop till, the app on the phone, the clip everybody is sharing. It is fun, but a lot of it is a small true thing with a big made-up story wrapped round it.
explain: A clever reader sorts every story into four piles: what is confirmed (the person said it, or trusted news checked it), what is blown up (true but made bigger), what is pure rumour, and what is simply private.
explain: Vivid example: in a Leeds corner shop, Auntie Bev holds up a magazine: "SOAP STAR'S SECRET HEARTBREAK! Look at her, all drawn at the airport!" Mum looks closer: "Bev, it's one photo of a tired woman at an airport. Everyone looks like that at six in the morning."
explain: Words like "sources close to", "friends fear" and "everyone says" often mean one unnamed person, or nobody at all. And behind every story is a real person who may read it too.
fact: A tabloid is a newspaper or magazine that sells by shocking headlines and gossip about famous people; one ordinary photo plus dramatic words can become a whole story.
fact: "Sources close to" means the paper is not naming who told it; it can be a real friend, a guess or nobody, and the reader cannot check.
fact: Death hoaxes about famous people spread fast because they are shocking; check a proper news site before believing or sharing one.
fact: Magazines that circle bits of a star's body in red are mocking an ordinary body, and teach readers to feel ashamed of their own.
fact: Paparazzi are photographers who follow famous people to sell pictures; chasing someone home is frightening and dangerous, and no photo is worth that.
fact: A quote stuck on a picture of a famous face is very easy to fake; look for where the person really said it.
fact: "Must have been sacked" and "gone into rehab" (a clinic to stop drinking or drugs) are often guesses invented to explain a simple announcement like leaving a show or taking a break.
fact: A pregnancy, a marriage or a break-up is private until the people themselves choose to share it; fan "clues" like a ring in a photo are not facts.
fact: A picture without its story can lie: a "mugshot" can be from a music video, and a short viral clip can leave out what happened before and after.
fact: Some years ago some British newspaper journalists secretly listened to famous people's phone messages, which is against the law; they were caught, and it became a huge scandal.
fact: "Everyone says so" in a gossip story usually means one unnamed person said it.
fact: You can think a footballer's pay is unfair without hating the person who gets it, and making videos online is a kind of work too.
exercise: The magazine says "friends fear" for a star "looking drawn at the airport". What is actually confirmed? | A photographer took one picture of her at an airport | Her friends have told the magazine they are very scared for her | She has announced she is heartbroken | Doctors say she is very unwell
exercise: "Sources close to the singer say she is splitting up." What does "sources close to" mean? | Somebody unnamed, who the reader cannot check | The singer herself, speaking to the paper on the record | Her manager, in an official statement | A court that has looked at the facts
exercise: A kid at school yells, "That famous footballer DIED, it's all over TikTok!" What should you do first? | Check a proper news site before believing it | Share it fast so everyone knows | Post a sad message on his page | Believe it, because so many people have already seen it
exercise: A magazine circles a star's tummy in red: "Shocking beach body!" What is really shocking here? | Mocking an ordinary body on holiday | The way she chose to stand there | The size of the beach in the photo | That she went to the beach at all
exercise: Mum says, "Twenty paparazzi chased that actress home on motorbikes." Which part is most likely true? | Photographers really did follow her home | Exactly twenty motorbikes were counted by the police | She asked them to follow her car | The chase was part of a film
exercise: Big brother: "That YouTuber's been cancelled over an old tweet!" Which is the fairest view? | Old mistakes matter, but so do apologies and change | Nothing anyone posted ten years ago matters at all now | He must now be hated forever by everybody | Only the number of unsubscribers matters
exercise: Nan sniffs at a chef's TV apology: "Crocodile tears." How can you judge an apology fairly? | By whether he names what he did and then changes | By how many tears you can see on screen during the video | By whether Nan likes him or not | By how long the apology goes on for
exercise: A fan page says a singer is having twins and shows a "scan". What should you think? | It is a rumour; she has said nothing herself | It must be true, there is a picture | It is fine to share as long as you're excited | The scan proves it, scans can't be faked
exercise: Two pop stars stop following each other online. Is that a feud? | Not necessarily; fans turn small things into wars | Yes, unfollowing someone always means a big fight behind it | Yes, and the song proves it for sure | No, they must be secretly married
exercise: A picture shows an actor's face with a horrible quote about nurses. What should you look for? | Where and when he really said it | How many people have liked it so far | Whether the photo of his face is nice | Whether nurses have seen it already
exercise: A TV presenter announces she is leaving her show. Auntie Jen: "Must've been sacked for something awful." What is that? | An invented scandal to explain a simple announcement | A confirmed fact from the TV channel that employed her | A polite way of saying she retired | What the presenter said herself
exercise: Big brother shows a "mugshot" of a rapper. It turns out to be from his music video. What is the lesson? | A picture without its story can tell a lie | Rappers are often arrested for making music videos | Music videos are always real arrests | Mugshots are taken for every film
exercise: Auntie Bev reads that a comedian has "gone into rehab". He actually said he was taking a break from touring. What is true? | Only that he is taking a break | That he is seriously ill and in hospital | That the paper spoke to his doctor | That he lied to fans about his break
exercise: A fan app says a boy band singer is "secretly married" because of a ring in a photo. What is it? | A fan theory built from one clue | A fact confirmed by his own family | A wedding announcement from the band | News checked by a proper newspaper
exercise: Mum says some British papers "hacked famous people's phones". What happened? | Journalists illegally listened to phone messages and got caught | Famous people let papers read their messages in return for good stories | Hackers sold phones to the newspapers | Phones were broken by the papers on purpose
exercise: A gossip site quotes "a crew member" saying a film star is a nightmare. Auntie Jen says "everyone says so". How many people said it? | One unnamed person | Everyone who worked on the film | The film star's own family | A whole court full of witnesses
exercise: Grandad: "Influencers never do a day's work!" Which is fairer? | Making content is work, even if it looks strange | Influencers are all paid a wage by the government | Grandad is right, it's no work at all | Influencers only post once a year
test: Big sister, scrolling: "omg 'sources close to' her say she's dumped him!!" You ask what the singer herself said. "Nothing yet." So how sure is it? | Not sure at all; nobody has confirmed it | Totally sure, because sources never lie | Sure, because it's on a big app | Sure, because all her fans believe it
test: Your mate: "he's DEAD, swear down, my cousin sent me a TikTok." Then the footballer posts a training video an hour later. What was it? | A death hoax that spread because it was shocking | A real death that his club is hiding from the fans | A mistake by the BBC news team | A video made after he died
test: Dad grumbles, "Footballer gets in a week what I get in ten years. Bloody disgusting." Which view is fair? | You can think the pay unfair without hating him | He should give all his money to Dad | Footballers choose their own pay every week, as much as they like | Dad must be lying about his pay
test: Auntie Bev: "Look at her face, she's had work done, she looks nothing like in that film." The film was twenty years ago. What is most likely? | She is older now and wearing make-up | She has certainly had an operation | The magazine used a different woman | She is wearing a mask on purpose
test: Kids at school: "A famous footballer's moving here! My cousin's mate's dad's building his house!" Why is it hard to trust? | The chain of people makes it impossible to check | Footballers are not allowed to move house during the season | Builders never talk about their jobs | Cousins always tell the exact truth
test: Nan, shocked: "That newsreader's left her husband for the weatherman! It's in all the magazines." The photo shows them leaving a restaurant together. What is confirmed? | Only that they left a restaurant at the same time | That they are in love and moving in together next month | That her husband has said it's true | That the weatherman has resigned
test: Dad: "Politician caught on camera saying summat stupid at a party." What should you do before sharing the clip? | Watch the whole clip, the headline may twist it | Share it straight away while it's still trending everywhere | Add your own caption to make it funnier | Believe the headline without watching
test: Big brother: "Look, streamer screamed at a kid in his chat, clip's going viral!" What's missing from the clip? | What happened just before and just after | The streamer's name and his face | Any sound at all from the stream | The number of people watching it
test: Little sister: "The papers can write anything they want about famous people, it's allowed." Put her right. | No, they must not lie or break privacy laws | Yes, famous people gave up all their rights to privacy | Yes, as long as it's printed in a glossy magazine | No, papers can only write nice things about people
test: Auntie Bev hands you a mag: "Star's SHOCKING beach body!! look at her belly lol." What does a kind reader take from it? | An ordinary body is being mocked, and that's unkind | Her belly really is the biggest news story of the week | It's fine, because she's famous and rich | She must have wanted the photo taken
test: Mum: "Paparazzi chased her home with her kids in the car." Why is this not just "part of being famous"? | It is frightening and puts people in danger | Famous people enjoy being chased home by cameras | Her kids secretly wanted to be in all the papers | It only ever happens in films, never in real life
test: Auntie Jen: "That star's a total nightmare, everyone says, it was on a gossip site." What does "everyone says" usually mean? | One person said it, without a name | Every person on the planet agrees | The star said it about himself | A judge decided it in court
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of a report attributed solely to "sources close to" a public figure. | The origin of the claim cannot be verified by the reader | The report must by law be entirely false | The figure concerned has personally and publicly confirmed the claim | The report has been checked by a court
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the interception of telephone messages by certain British newspapers. | It was unlawful, was exposed, and led to a public inquiry | It was lawful, and was approved by those affected | It never occurred, being a rumour of the internet | It was performed solely by foreign governments
exam: Whereas a photograph of a public figure is published without any account of its circumstances, determine the conclusion which this lesson commends. | That the image alone may mislead as to what occurred | That the image alone proves the accompanying headline | That the image must have been taken by the figure | That the image is necessarily a forgery
exam: Ascertain the course of action most appropriate upon encountering an unverified report of the death of a celebrated sportsman upon a social media platform. | Consultation of a reputable news source before belief or sharing | Immediate distribution of the report to all acquaintances | Acceptance of the report on account of its popularity | Publication of condolences upon the sportsman's page
